<section class="box-exercise"><header><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Analytical_Chemistry_2.1_(Harvey)/09:_Titrimetric_Methods/9.03:_Complexation_Titrations" class="mt-section"><span id="Exercise_9.3.1"></span><h5 id="Exercise_9.3.1-165346"><span class="lt-icon-default">Exercise 9.3.1
</span></h5></div></header>

<p class="lt-chem-165346">Calculate titration curves for the titration of 50.0 mL of \(5.00 \times 10^{-3}\) M Cd<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup> with 0.0100 M EDTA (a) at a pH of 10 and (b) at a pH of 7. Neither titration includes an auxiliary complexing agent. Compare your results with Figure 9.3.3
 and comment on the effect of pH on the titration of Cd<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup> with EDTA.</p>

<dl>
    <dt><strong class="emphasis bold">Answer</strong></dt>
    <dd>
    <p class="lt-chem-165346">Let&rsquo;s begin with the calculations at a pH of 10 where some of the EDTA is present in forms other than Y<sup>4&ndash;</sup>. To evaluate the titration curve, therefore, we need the conditional formation constant for CdY<sup>2&ndash;</sup>, which, from <a title="9.3: Complexation Titrations" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Analytical_Chemistry_2.1_(Harvey)/09%3A_Titrimetric_Methods/9.03%3A_Complexation_Titrations#table9.3.2" rel="internal">Table 9.3.2
</a> is \(K_f^{\prime} = 1.1 \times 10^{16}\). Note that the conditional formation constant is larger in the absence of an auxiliary complexing agent.</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">The titration&rsquo;s equivalence point requires</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">\[V_{e q}=V_{\mathrm{EDTA}}=\frac{M_{\mathrm{Cd}} V_{\mathrm{Cd}}}{M_{\mathrm{EDTA}}}=\frac{\left(5.00 \times 10^{-3} \ \mathrm{M}\right)(50.0 \ \mathrm{mL})}{(0.0100 \ \mathrm{M})}=25.0 \ \mathrm{mL} \nonumber\]</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">of EDTA.</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">Before the equivalence point, Cd<sup>2+</sup> is present in excess and pCd is determined by the concentration of unreacted Cd<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>. For example, after adding 5.00 mL of EDTA, the total concentration of Cd<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup> is</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">\[\left[\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\right]=\frac{\left(5.00 \times 10^{-3} \ \mathrm{M}\right)(50.0 \ \mathrm{mL})-(0.0100 \ \mathrm{M})(5.00 \ \mathrm{mL})}{50.0 \ \mathrm{mL}+5.00 \ \mathrm{mL}} \nonumber\]</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">which gives [Cd<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>] as \(3.64 \times 10^{-3}\) and pCd as 2.43.</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">At the equivalence point all Cd<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup> initially in the titrand is now present as CdY<sup>2&ndash;</sup>. The concentration of Cd<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>, therefore, is determined by the dissociation of the CdY<sup>2&ndash;</sup> complex. First, we calculate the concentration of CdY<sup>2&ndash;</sup>.</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">\[\left[\mathrm{CdY}^{2-}\right]=\frac{\left(5.00 \times 10^{-3} \ \mathrm{M}\right)(50.0 \ \mathrm{mL})}{50.0 \ \mathrm{mL}+25.00 \ \mathrm{mL}}=3.33 \times 10^{-3} \ \mathrm{M} \nonumber\]</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">Next, we solve for the concentration of Cd<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup> in equilibrium with CdY<sup>2&ndash;</sup>.</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">\[K_{f}^{\prime}=\frac{\left[\mathrm{CdY}^{2-}\right]}{\left[\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\right] C_{\mathrm{EDTA}}}=\frac{3.33 \times 10^{-3}-x}{(x)(x)}=1.1 \times 10^{16} \nonumber\]</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">Solving gives [Cd<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>] as \(5.50 \times 10^{-10}\) M or a pCd of 9.26 at the equivalence point.</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">After the equivalence point, EDTA is in excess and the concentration of Cd<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup> is determined by the dissociation of the CdY<sup>2&ndash;</sup> complex. First, we calculate the concentrations of CdY<sup>2&ndash;</sup> and of unreacted EDTA. For example, after adding 30.0 mL of EDTA</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">\[\left[\mathrm{CdY}^{2-}\right]=\frac{\left(5.00 \times 10^{-3} \ \mathrm{M}\right)(50.0 \ \mathrm{mL})}{50.0 \ \mathrm{mL}+30.00 \ \mathrm{mL}}=3.12 \times 10^{-3} \ \mathrm{M} \nonumber\]</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">\[C_{\mathrm{EDTA}}=\frac{(0.0100 \ \mathrm{M})(30.00 \ \mathrm{mL})-\left(5.00 \times 10^{-3} \ \mathrm{M}\right)(50.0 \ \mathrm{mL})}{50.0 \ \mathrm{mL}+30.00 \ \mathrm{mL}} \nonumber\]</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">\[C_{\mathrm{EDTA}}=6.25 \times 10^{-4} \ \mathrm{M} \nonumber\]</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">Substituting into the equation for the conditional formation constant</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">\[K_{f}^{\prime}=\frac{\left[\mathrm{CdY}^{2-}\right]}{\left[\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\right] C_{\mathrm{EDTA}}}=\frac{3.12 \times 10^{-3} \ \mathrm{M}}{(\mathrm{x})\left(6.25 \times 10^{-4} \ \mathrm{M}\right)}=1.1 \times 10^{16} \nonumber\]</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">and solving for [Cd<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup>] gives \(4.54 \times 10^{-16}\) M or a pCd of 15.34.</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-165346">The calculations at a pH of 7 are identical, except the conditional formation constant for CdY<sup>2&ndash;</sup> is \(1.5 \times 10^{13}\) instead of \(1.1 \times 10^{16}\). The following table summarizes results for these two titrations as well as the results from <a title="9.3: Complexation Titrations" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Analytical_Chemistry_2.1_(Harvey)/09%3A_Titrimetric_Methods/9.03%3A_Complexation_Titrations#table9.3.4" rel="internal">Table 9.3.4
</a> for the titration of Cd<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup> at a pH of 10 in the presence of 0.0100 M NH<sub>3</sub> as an auxiliary complexing agent.</p>

